# Static arm configuration. The orchestrator never changes these per ticket.
# Copy this file to .env and fill in the paths below.

# Host paths to two checkouts of the same commit. These are the bind-mount
# sources and where the harness runs its own file ops (artifacts, review).
CONTROL_REPO=/absolute/path/to/control-checkout
GREPTILE_REPO=/absolute/path/to/greptile-checkout

# Container isolation. Each arm's codex runs via `docker exec` inside its own
# container so it cannot read the other arm's checkout or the host. Bring the
# containers up with scripts/arm-run.sh (which mounts each checkout at
# /workspace), then leave these set — this is the standard way to run the
# harness. Unset them only for a throwaway local smoke test, where both arms
# share the host filesystem and provide no isolation.
CONTROL_CONTAINER=vivarium-control
GREPTILE_CONTAINER=vivarium-greptile
# CONTROL_WORKSPACE=/workspace   # codex cwd inside the container (default)
# GREPTILE_WORKSPACE=/workspace

# GitHub token each arm authenticates with (its identity when opening PRs).
# scripts/arm-run.sh reads these from here and passes them into the container,
# so the token never appears on the command line. The HARNESS reads them too:
# it finds, reviews and merges each arm's pull request with the same token, so
# every action on a repo is attributed to that arm rather than to whoever
# started the run. Unset falls back to the host's `gh` auth (and starts the
# container with no GitHub auth at all, which means the arm cannot open a PR —
# and a subticket with no merged PR is a failed subticket).
# CONTROL_GH_TOKEN=ghp_...
# GREPTILE_GH_TOKEN=ghp_...

# Where each containerized arm's Codex transcripts land on the host, so the
# harness can copy them into the run artifacts. Both arm-run.sh (which mounts
# <dir>/sessions into the container) and the harness read this; it defaults to
# ~/.vivarium/<container-name>. Set it only to relocate that directory.
# CONTROL_CODEX_HOME=/absolute/path/to/.vivarium/vivarium-control
# GREPTILE_CODEX_HOME=/absolute/path/to/.vivarium/vivarium-greptile

# Sandbox for both arms' Codex sessions. Leave it unset: a containerized arm
# then runs danger-full-access, which is what it needs to push a branch, open a
# pull request and answer a review (the container, not the sandbox, is the
# isolation boundary), and a host arm runs workspace-write. Set this only to
# override both arms — workspace-write blocks the network, so a containerized
# run under it cannot land anything.
# CODEX_SANDBOX=danger-full-access

# Where to find Codex sessions, for copying transcripts. Defaults to ~/.codex.
# CODEX_HOME=/absolute/path/to/.codex

# Abort a Codex session after this much event silence (activity watchdog).
# Defaults to 600000 (10m); 0 disables. Lower it if hung external tool calls
# should be cut off faster.
# IDLE_TIMEOUT_MS=240000

# Review rounds. After the greptile arm opens its pull request, the harness
# waits for this login to review it, then sends the arm back to fetch the
# comments itself and answer every one of them on the record before the pull
# request is merged. The control arm has no reviewer and merges straight away —
# that difference is the experiment. A review that never arrives does not hold
# the climb: after REVIEW_TIMEOUT_MS the pull request merges unreviewed and the
# timeout is recorded as the round's outcome.
# GREPTILE_BOT_LOGIN=greptile-apps[bot]
# REVIEW_TIMEOUT_MS=900000
# REVIEW_ROUNDS=2

# Linear filing. After Greg plans a rung, the LOOP files it in Linear
# mechanically over Linear's hosted MCP: a project milestone "Rung N — Title"
# under LINEAR_PROJECT, an issue "[RUNG N.x] Title" per subticket attached to
# it, and blocking relations chaining the ordered steps. Issue ids are stamped
# onto the ladder headings. When a subticket's build succeeds, its issue is
# moved to Done — and unlike filing, that step fails CLOSED: a close that
# cannot be performed halts the run so the board never silently drifts from
# the ladder. Greg's own Codex session never files tickets — headless codex
# blocks destructive MCP tool calls on an approval nobody can answer. Leave
# these unset to skip Linear entirely. LINEAR_TEAM is the team name or id
# issues are filed under; LINEAR_PROJECT holds the rung milestones (without
# it, issues are filed unattached).
# LINEAR_API_KEY=lin_api_...
# LINEAR_TEAM=My Team
# LINEAR_PROJECT=My Project

# Everything else is fixed in code, not configured here: results land in
# ./results, arms get 3 attempts, the ladder pauses every 2 milestones, and
# Greg Tile (the default mode of `bun start`) adds no configuration of its own —
# it reuses the arms above and files a milestone plus a sub-issue per subticket
# via the `linear` MCP server.
